</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c6c3eb90-ddc3-11f1-b229-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-11T103442Z-1140154397-RC2MLOAZZVPK-RTRMADP-3-REMEMBRANCE-DAY-FRANCE-MACRON.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c6c3eb90-ddc3-11f1-b229-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-11T103442Z-1140154397-RC2MLOAZZVPK-RTRMADP-3-REMEMBRANCE-DAY-FRANCE-MACRON.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">b3d4b4a2-ddc2-11f1-91ff-a57a90c60791</guid><pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 11:36:53 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Eisenkot quickly targets Netanyahu's legacy in new Israeli parliament</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/eisenkot-quickly-targets-netanyahu-s-legacy-in-new-israeli-parliament</link><description>
Israeli ex-army chief Gadi Eisenkot and his allies launched a legislative onslaught against caretaker Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu's legacy on Tuesday, as a new parliament convened in a heated first session.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8491a9b4-ddb0-11f1-897d-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261111-080849-081212-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8491a9b4-ddb0-11f1-897d-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261111-080849-081212-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">895e049c-ddb0-11f1-a157-7b99786cc99c</guid><pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 11:29:28 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>EU ready for 'united response' after France blames Iran for fridge hacks</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/eu-ready-for-united-response-after-france-blames-iran-for-fridge-hacks</link><description>
The EU said Tuesday it would look to provide a "swift and united response" after France blamed Iran for a cyberattack on hundreds of supermarket fridges.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/be4cb28e-ddb0-11f1-b909-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261111-081243-081432-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/be4cb28e-ddb0-11f1-b909-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261111-081243-081432-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c31debb6-ddb0-11f1-a0af-8fd2c8b9e330</guid><pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 11:16:36 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>'France has some of the cleanest electricity in the world, but we're not using enough of it'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france-has-some-of-the-cleanest-electricity-in-the-world-but-we-re-not-using-enough-of-it</link><description><![CDATA[
Eve Irvine is pleased to welcome Thierry Bros, energy & climate expert and Professor of Science and Economics at Sciences Po. As geopolitical conflicts unsettle global energy markets and governments struggle to reconcile climate commitments with economic and security imperatives, the answer lies in a fundamental reconsideration of the sequence of climate policy. "Electrification first," he argues, acknowledging that this "doesn't necessarily mean clean electricity." His argument is grounded in a critical assessment of international climate diplomacy and European energy policy. "We've seen many COPs, and they've all failed," he contends, questioning the effectiveness of ambitious targets that have yet to deliver the necessary structural transformation. In his view, electrification offers a more pragmatic route than some of the technological priorities previously championed by European policymakers.
]]></description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f1e4adc8-dd50-11f1-af77-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261110-080851-081548-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f1e4adc8-dd50-11f1-af77-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261110-080851-081548-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f73052dc-dd50-11f1-b4b8-1bd9e2b61578</guid><pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 11:12:15 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><title>🔴Italy: At least 300 schools evacuated after email bomb alerts</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261111-hundreds-of-schools-in-italy-evacuated-after-receiving-bomb-threats</link><description></description><guid isPermaLink="false">ca070ecc-ddbe-11f1-ab4e-ada4b5152abb</guid><pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:59:06 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Wave of hoax bomb threats sent to schools in UK, Ireland and Italy</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261111-hundreds-of-schools-in-italy-evacuated-after-receiving-bomb-threats</link><description>
Hundreds of schools in the UK, Italy and the Republic of Ireland received a "generic" threatening email on Wednesday now being treated as a hoax by authorities. Italy earlier evacuated roughly 300 schools over the perceived threat. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/37b21574-ddcf-11f1-b10f-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D76J9ZD.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/37b21574-ddcf-11f1-b10f-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D76J9ZD.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">abeebfd4-ddbe-11f1-837a-4118e40710b0</guid><pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:57:35 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/37b21574-ddcf-11f1-b10f-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D76J9ZD.jpg">© Stefano Rellandini, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Pope Leo to end Argentina tour with outdoor mass before Peru 'homecoming'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261111-pope-leo-to-end-argentina-tour-with-large-outdoor-mass-before-emotional-peru-homecoming</link><description>
Pope Leo will conclude his three-day visit to Argentina Wednesday with an outdoor mass expected to draw millions before continuing his tour of South America by travelling to Peru, where he once served as a missionary. The pontiff plans to deliver a speech in front of Peruvian President Keiko Fujimori, whose election earlier this year was marred ‌by fraud accusations and vote-counting delays.
